          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mix the flour, baking powder, sugar, spices and dried fruit in a large bowl. Rub in the grated butter until the mixture resembles coarse breadcrumbs."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Stir in the treacle and beaten egg. Mix to a firm, slightly sticky dough. Add a tablespoon of water if it seems too dry."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Dip a large square of cotton cloth in boiling water. Wring it out and lay it flat. Dust the centre generously with flour. Place the dumpling mixture in the centre and gather the cloth up, tying it firmly with string, leaving a little room for the pudding to swell."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Put a plate in the base of the stockpot to stop the cloth touching the bottom. Lower the dumpling in and cover with boiling water. Cover and simmer for 3 hours, topping up with boiling water as needed."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Lift out the dumpling and unwrap it carefully. Place in a low oven at 150°C for 10 minutes to dry the skin. Serve hot with custard or cold in slices fried in butter."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "The skin has not formed properly.",
              "solution": "The cloth was not well-floured before wrapping. The flour layer is what forms the characteristic dumpling skin. Dust more generously next time."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "The dumpling is still wet in the centre after 3 hours.",
              "solution": "The water may have been at a low simmer. Make sure it is a proper, steady simmer throughout. A larger dumpling may need an extra 30 minutes."
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshooterItem",
            "attrs": {
              "problem": "The cloth stuck to the dumpling when unwrapping.",
              "solution": "Peel carefully and slowly. The skin is fragile when hot. The cloth lifts more easily if you let the dumpling rest for 5 minutes first."
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Clootie dumpling is tied to Scottish celebration food. It appears at Hogmanay, Burns Night and birthdays across the country. Coins or charms were traditionally buried inside for luck. Cold leftover slices fried in butter for breakfast the next day are considered by many Scots to be better than the pudding itself."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
